Nectar (drink):
{{Short description|Type of non-carbonated soft drink}}
{{About|drink|sugar-rich liquid produced by flowering plants|Nectar|other uses|Nectar (disambiguation)}}
{{refimprove|date=October 2013}}

'''Nectars''' are a type of soft drink made by muddling the flesh of fruits or an alcoholic drink made from the fermentation of honey.

== Nectar (soft drink) ==
'''Nectars''' are a type of non-carbonated [[soft drink]] made by [[muddler|muddling]] the flesh of fruits{{fact|date=January 2018}}

In some countries, the [[drink industry|beverage industry]] distinguishes nectars from drinks labeled as "juice". In the [[United States]] and the [[United Kingdom]], the term "fruit juice" is restricted to beverages that are 100% pure juice, whereas a "nectar" may be diluted (to a degree limited by regulations) with water and contain [[food additive|additive]]s besides fruit juice, including natural and artificial [[Sugar substitute|sweeteners]], and [[preservative]]s.<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=Pi46AAAAIAAJ&dq=nectar+drink+regulations&pg=PA342 ''The Code of Federal Regulations of the United States of America'']</ref> In [[New Zealand]], the usage is reversed, with "juice" denoting a sweetened fruit drink, whereas nectar refers to pure fruit.

== Nectar (alcoholic drink) ==
Nectars are a variant of [[mead]], alcohol fermented from honey. They are typically fermented to below 6% [[Alcohol by volume|ABV]], they often incorporate other flavours such as [[Fruit|fruits]], herbs and [[Spice|spices]]. 

